**Rheumatoid arthritis (RA)** is an **autoimmune** condition. The immune system, which normally fights infection, attacks the lining of the joints, causing pain and swelling. Over time it can damage the joints. Around **450,000 adults** in the UK have it. It is 2 to 3 times more common in women and often starts between 30 and 60.

## Symptoms

- **pain, swelling and stiffness** in the joints, often the **hands, wrists and feet**
- usually on **both sides** of the body
- stiffness that is **worse in the morning** and lasts more than 30 minutes
- extreme **tiredness** (fatigue)
- feeling generally unwell, with a high temperature or weight loss

Symptoms often come and go. A worsening of symptoms is called a **flare**.

## Getting diagnosed

Early treatment makes a big difference, so NICE says people with suspected RA should be referred **urgently** to a rheumatology specialist. Tests may include:

- **blood tests**, such as rheumatoid factor, anti-CCP antibodies and markers of inflammation (CRP and ESR)
- **X-rays** or an **ultrasound** of the joints

Read about [treatment for rheumatoid arthritis](/conditions/musculoskeletal/rheumatoid-arthritis/treatment/).
